{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MbEasQD4xzL9sDsLXk9WyA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01L2HRLM4QjrLshbgFjokp6w","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","subagent_type":"claude","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. Review the following paper independently and produce a structured review.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"The Generational Discrimination Boundary in Recursive Synthetic Text: K* as a Model-Family-Dependent Characterization Quantity\"\n\nThe paper introduces K*, the generational discrimination boundary — the maximum depth at which successive generations of recursive synthetic text remain statistically distinguishable. Key claims:\n- C1: K* ∈ {1,2,3} depending on (architecture, scorer, representation), with K*=2 universal under within-family scoring, K*=1 under cross-family mismatch\n- C2: K*≤3 is a DPI-bounded universal upper limit\n- C3: K*=2 predicts binary filtering as optimal data curation strategy\n\n## Method Details\n\n**Recursive Generation Protocol**: Two mechanisms:\n- Prompt-chain (canonical): seed text x0 → model generates x1 → x2 → ... to depth 5. Nucleus sampling p=0.95, T=1.0 default, with ablations.\n- Retrain-chain: LoRA fine-tuning (rank=16, α=32) on depth-k text to produce depth-(k+1). More ecologically valid.\n\n**Feature Extraction**: 15D feature vector per text sample:\n- Surprisal statistics (7): mean, std, skewness of token-level surprisal under scorer; first/second-order differences\n- Lexical diversity (4): TTR, hapax ratio, freq entropy, freq-rank slope\n- Structural (4): sentence length mean/var, punctuation density, repetition ratio\n\n**K* Determination**: LightGBM binary classifier on 15D features per consecutive depth pair (k, k+1). AUC with threshold θ=0.60, bootstrap 95% CI (1000 resamples), permutation testing. K* = max{k : AUC ≥ θ AND CI_lower ≥ θ}.\n\n**Filtering Strategies**: No filter, Binary (remove depth≥1), Graduated (α^d weighting), DSIR baseline.\n\n## Experiments Conducted\n\n1. **Cross-family scoring (LLaMA-8B, Mistral-7B)**: Both K*=1 with Qwen-1.5B scorer. 0v1 AUC >0.97 but 1v2 AUC 0.578/0.569 < θ.\n2. **4×4 cross-scorer matrix**: 4 generators × 4 scorers. Overall 10/16 (62.5%) K*=2. Within-family 10/10 (100%) K*=2. Cross-family 6/12 (50%).\n3. **Decoding ablation (Pythia-1.4B)**: Greedy & nucleus p=0.9 → K*=2; T=0.7 & T=1.2 → K*=1. 2/4 K*=2.\n4. **Text length ablation**: 128 tokens → K*=1; 256/512/~400 → K*=2.\n5. **Retrain-chain (Pythia-1.4B LoRA)**: K*=1, vs prompt-chain K*=2.\n6. **Domain ablation (Wikipedia)**: K*=2 marginal (point estimate 0.606, CI lower 0.595 < θ).\n7. **Dose-response**: Binary > Graduated at all doses (10/30/50%).\n8. **Naturalistic validation (Common Crawl)**: Correlational only, ρ=-0.31 with AlignScore.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (what was planned)\n\nThe experiment design specified these planned experiments:\n- exp-018: LLaMA-3.1-8B cross-family (K*=1 expected) — COVERED in paper\n- exp-019: Mistral-7B cross-family (K*=1 expected) — COVERED in paper\n- exp-020: Pythia-1.4B decoding ablation (2/4 K*=2 expected) — COVERED in paper\n- exp-021: Text length ablation 128/256/512 (K*=1 at 128) — COVERED in paper\n- exp-022: 4×4 cross-scorer matrix (62.5% K*=2) — COVERED in paper\n- exp-008: Retrain-chain ecological validity (K*=1) — COVERED in paper\n- exp-009: Contamination dose-response — COVERED in paper\n- exp-023: Domain ablation Wikipedia (K*=2 marginal) — COVERED in paper\n- exp-014: Naturalistic validation Common Crawl — COVERED in paper\n\nAll planned experiments appear covered.\n\n## Your Task\n\nEvaluate **methods** and **experiments** along these dimensions:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the K* determination procedure (LightGBM + AUC threshold) well-justified? Are there methodological concerns?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Bootstrap CIs, permutation tests, threshold choice (θ=0.60). Are these adequate?\n3. **Experimental coverage**: Are the experiments sufficient to support the claims? Any critical gaps?\n4. **Experimental design quality**: Controls, baselines, ablations — are they appropriate?\n5. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce?\n6. **Weaknesses in experiments**: Any confounds, missing controls, questionable choices?\n\nProvide your review in this format:\n\n### R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n**Summary**: (2-3 sentences)\n\n**Strengths**:\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- ...\n\n**Weaknesses**:\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- ...\n\n**Questions for Authors**:\n- Q1: ...\n- ...\n\n**Minor Issues**:\n- ...\n\n**Soundness Score** (1-4): \n**Confidence** (1-5):\n**Preliminary Score** (1-6, where 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept):\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":804,"cache_read_input_tokens":56385,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":804},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0cd65a7e-15e5-494f-a2d8-b1924e4b8500","uuid":"93134c72-298f-46d2-a352-7ac4622624ea"}
